## Series context & the key point _(why this concept, not a generic one)_

1.1 installed the *why* (UV ≈ 80% of visible aging, on a finite scaffold; win it outside-in and in proportion). 2.0 turned the topical 10% into a daily working drawing on the face. 2.1 cut behind the wall to the systemic + machine routes. **3.0 is the capstone reveal: that whole stack was built for one small district — the face — and the reader has been ignoring the rest of the plot.** The article's framing is the thesis: *"skin on the face and skin on the body are two different organs in practice,"* and the highest-leverage line in the build plan — *"the neck, hands and décolletage are where age shows first and worst, precisely because people sunscreen their face and stop at the jaw."*

The article makes six structural moves the poster must carry, but only the first two are the **hero**; the rest dock as zoned callouts and a spec sheet:

1. **THE central architecture — the body is a different organ, mapped by zone.** Not one uniform surface but a *territory* with a gland-density / behaviour map: oily, acne-prone **back / chest / shoulders**; dry, flaking / KP-prone **shins / forearms**; thicker stratum corneum everywhere (slower turnover, slower healing). The mechanisms from 1.1 / 2.0 carry over — *this article is about where they change below the neck.* Read as a **zoning survey of a single human figure**, each region tagged with its dominant issue and its body-specific fix.
2. **THE JAW LINE — care stops at the jaw; aging does not.** ==The load-bearing insight.== People over-maintain the face and abandon everything below it, so the **chronically sun-exposed, routine-neglected border zones — neck, back-of-hands, décolletage — reveal age first and worst.** This pays off 1.1's *"~80% photoaging"* thesis directly: *the hands are the proof.* The fix is *almost free* — **extend the face routine down past the jaw** (SPF the neck + hands daily, bring retinoid + moisturizer down). This must be drawn as a literal **boundary line across the body** where the dense "maintenance" hatching of the whole prior series stops dead — while the sun keeps crossing it.
3. **Body acne — back & chest ("bacne"), and the fungal trap.** Distinguish (a) **true bacterial / hormonal acne** — benzoyl-peroxide wash, salicylic, ± the 2.1 systemic options — from (b) ==**fungal acne / Malassezia folliculitis**, rampant in the tropical sweat-and-humidity context, which does NOT respond to acne treatment and needs an antifungal.== Sweat hygiene (shower post-gym, breathable fabric) is the 90%. This is the article's sharpest "treating it wrong makes it worse" rule and the natural home for one rationed red — the exact twin of Skin 2.0's fungal-acne trap, now relocated to the back/chest zone.
4. **Keratosis pilaris — the "chicken skin" arms.** Very common, benign, keratin plugging; *manage, don't cure.* **Urea 10–20% / lactic / salicylic body lotion, gentle exfoliation, moisturize — don't scrub raw.** (OTC doses fine to print, per house convention — this and the body routine are where real numbers may appear.)
5. **The body routine — retinoid, urea, SPF.** Body retinoid works below the neck too (tone, texture, KP, photoaging) at cheaper body-grade, same PM / photolabile / ramp rules as the face. **Urea = the body-moisturizer workhorse.** Body SPF on the exposed zones everyone skips. The **minimum effective routine** is deliberately short: *gentle wash → urea/moisturize → SPF on exposed skin → BP/antifungal wash as needed → optional body retinoid PM.* Don't over-build; the body's 90% is *sun + sweat hygiene + moisturize.*
6. **Stretch marks — honest expectation-setting.** Dermal collagen/elastin tears. ***Striae rubrae*** (red / early) respond *somewhat* (retinoid, possibly the 2.1 microneedling/laser ladder); ***striae albae*** (white / mature) are **largely permanent — manage, don't promise.** A small condemnation-style "the window closes" device, in-voice and anti-hype — the cousin of Hair 1.1's fibrosis line.

**The single thing a reader must walk away owning:** *The face is one small over-tended district; the body is a different organ you've been ignoring. Aging doesn't stop at the jaw even though your sunscreen does — so the neck and the backs of your hands betray your age first, and they are the cheapest fix you're not making: just run the line down. Below the jaw, match the zone to the issue (oily back → BP, and check it isn't fungal before you treat it as acne; dry KP arms → urea, manage not cure; everywhere → sun + sweat hygiene + moisturize), keep the routine lean, and tell the truth about stretch marks — the red ones have a window, the white ones don't.*

That is **a whole-body zoning survey split by a single abandoned care-boundary, with zone-matched fixes, one fungal interlock, and one closing-window verdict** — which is why the honest anchor is, once more, the architect's drawing, but now the **widest cut in the series: the full site-survey of the body plot, the jaw line where care stopped, and the sun that never read the memo.**

