## 6. Style Reference

The locked Attractive signature: **"Architect's Elevation / Spec Sheet"** — a measured architectural survey on bone drafting paper, dimensioned callouts, stencil labels, single-alarm-red discipline, one boxed failure-state. Closest external touchstones: a building **site-survey / zoning plan**, an old **anatomical figure rendered as an engineering elevation**, the dry diagrammatic authority of *Wait But Why* crossed with an architect's plot drawing — never a skincare ad, never a glossy body shot. This poster is specifically the **widest-shot member** of the Skin set: 1.1 (wall section) → 2.0 (day schedule) → 2.1 (deep services) → **3.0 (the whole plot)** — same hand, camera pulled all the way back.

## 7. What to Avoid

- **The glossy full-body "after" shot / a flawless model torso.** This is the genericest possible move and it destroys the entire thesis — the article is anti-hype and the hero is a *boundary line*, not radiant skin. No dewy bodies.
- **A grid / menu of body-care products (washes, lotions, SPF bottles).** Flattens the zone-matching and the jaw-line argument into a shopping list; the whole point is *region → issue → fix* and *the line you're not running down*, not a product haul.
- **A neutral anatomy chart with evenly-weighted labels everywhere.** The composition must be *lopsided* — a tiny over-tended face above the line, a large neglected territory below, the neck/hands screaming. Equal-weight labeling kills the betrayal.
- **Red-flooding.** Keep alarm-red to the two earned marks (betrayal vector + fungal trap). The KP and stretch-mark cautions are amber. If everything is red, the fungal trap stops meaning "this one will actively backfire."
- **Over-building the body routine to match the face.** The footer must look *leaner* than 2.0's face schedule on purpose — drawing an equally elaborate body stack contradicts the article's "don't over-build; the body's 90% is sun + sweat + moisturize."
